assign='''Form is fixed before counts as one signed plenary merits opinion. The recurring previous-work construction, identified delegation and practical justification are one shared rationale; all nine support it and the bounded implementation mandate. Separate writings add no legal work.

'''+assignment(date,JUSTICES,'Souter',
'''Fit governs. Souter's Walton opinion applies the same adult-disability delegation to a functional eligibility screen while distinguishing permissible implementation from the only possible reading. That is the exact legal work here. Thomas, at zero while Souter has one, offers a strong alternative through Heimmermann's actual-mechanism analysis, but Souter's concrete work in this statutory scheme integrates the source of authority, claimant's textual objection and imperfect capacity proxy most directly. Stone-Zsela's approved position fully joins the result and rule; personal authorship supplies no superior precision or coordination advantage. Scalia's historical authorship does not enter this selection. No expansion is needed.''')
kernel='''The Court reviews the Third Circuit's legal requirement at 294 F.3d 568 that previous work be shown to exist in the national economy before a step-four denial. Thomas's individual elevator-operator job ended on August 25, 1995; nationwide occupational extinction was not found. The agency found her capable of the former work. The fresh preflight's source-based correction of the neutral packet preserves that asserted/found distinction without changing the approved legal question or Stone's core. `runtime/chunk2_a_sources/thomas_lower.txt` supplies the statutory and regulatory recitations and majority/dissent arguments. The final reconciliation verifies the historical opinion's three footnotes, including the September 25, 2003 renumbering of former step-four/five subsections; the public account therefore identifies the steps without obsolete subsection letters.

Entering law is Mead's actual-delegation-and-exercise rule, Walton's section 405(a) and SSI incorporation holdings, and Heimmermann's delegated mechanism within statutory limits. This decision applies those rules rather than enlarging general administrative authority. No earlier current-term decision changes the disability rule. Historical unanimity confirms the Associate commitments but supplies no authorship or argument date.

Stone's approved core supports the permissible prior-work construction, the claimant's step-four capacity burden, the agency's support obligation and ordinary substantial-evidence review of any remaining preserved capacity challenge. He joins the opinion and judgment in full. The mandate implements the valid step-four rule on the established capacity finding while retaining ordinary review of any independently preserved actual-capacity contention; it invents no such contention. His independent reading of the statutory room and his support for the identified delegated choice are both represented. No standing fallback, new burden or narrowing is used. All eight Associates' P1–P5 survive, including Scalia's refusal of independent weight from longevity or informality. No published noncontrolling ground or institutional change results.'''
blocks={
'Event':'''Barnhart v. Thomas; No. 02-763; 540 U.S. 20. Merits decision, November 12, 2003, on merits submission. On review of the Third Circuit's judgment, the Court considers whether the Social Security Administration may deny an adult disability claim because the claimant can perform past relevant work without determining that the occupation currently exists in significant numbers in the national economy. Thomas's elevator-operator position ended, and the agency found retained capacity to perform that work. The Commissioner seeks reversal of the additional national-existence requirement; Thomas seeks reconsideration under that requirement.''',
'Participation':'''Stone-Zsela, Stevens, O'Connor, Scalia, Kennedy, Souter, Thomas, Ginsburg and Breyer participate at submission and decision.''',
'Public Action':'''The Third Circuit's judgment is reversed and the case remanded to apply the permissible previous-work rule.''',
'Judgment & Remedy':'''**Reversed and remanded, 9–0.**

**Supporting:** Stone-Zsela, Stevens, O'Connor, Scalia, Kennedy, Souter, Thomas, Ginsburg and Breyer.

Thomas is not entitled to a separate national-existence inquiry as a prerequisite to step-four denial. The lower court must implement the valid rule on the established capacity finding and resolve any remaining preserved challenge to that finding under ordinary substantial-evidence review. If none succeeds, the step-four denial stands.''',
'Opinion Topology':'''SOUTER, J., delivered the opinion of the Court, in which STONE-ZSELA, C. J., and STEVENS, O'CONNOR, SCALIA, KENNEDY, THOMAS, GINSBURG, and BREYER, JJ., joined.

| Writing | Author | Joined by | Scope |
|---|---|---|---|
| Opinion of the Court | Souter | Stone-Zsela, Stevens, O'Connor, Scalia, Kennedy, Thomas, Ginsburg, Breyer | Whole statutory, delegation and reasonable-implementation rationale and remedy |''',
'Holdings':'''### Retained capacity for previous work may end the inquiry

**Controlling proposition:** Under the adult disability definitions in sections 423(d)(2)(A) and 1382c(a)(3)(B), the agency may deny a claim at step four when the claimant retains capacity to perform qualifying past relevant work, without separately proving that the occupation currently exists in significant numbers in the national economy. The claimant retains the applicable step-four burden; the agency must support the actual capacity finding and apply its valid regulations. National availability governs the subsequent other-work step.

**Authority:** Souter's opinion, joined by all eight other Justices; the Court sustains a permissible construction, not an exclusively mandated statutory interpretation.

**Controlling explanation:** The statute distinguishes inability to perform previous work from inability, considering age, education and work experience, to perform other substantial gainful work existing in the national economy. The word connecting those categories does not unambiguously carry the later qualification backward. The overall impairment-caused-inability definition leaves room for this separate capacity screen. Congress's exclusions of a particular vacancy, hiring likelihood and the claimant's immediate locality fit that structure, although those exclusions alone do not resolve nationwide obsolescence.

Section 405(a), incorporated for SSI through section 1383(d)(1), supplies the actual statutory assignment, and the binding five-step regulations exercise it. Mead requires both delegation and its actual exercise; Walton identifies both in this disability scheme, while Heimmermann preserves the enacted limits. Expertise, complexity or administrative longevity alone would not supply that authority.

Past relevant work demonstrates demands the claimant actually performed and offers a reasonable measure of retained functional ability. Thomas's strongest objection is that ability to do a vanished occupation may not establish ability to earn income in any existing job. That exposes the proxy's imperfect fit, but does not make the permissible distinction between impairment-caused incapacity and job disappearance irrational. Administrative practicality supports a choice the statute permits; it cannot override the statute. The required actual capacity and qualifying-past-work findings remain essential. The agency need not conduct the additional occupational-existence inquiry that the Third Circuit required.''',
'Precedent Treatment':'''- United States v. Mead Corp.: applied through identified congressional delegation and exercise in binding regulations, preserving its distinction between administrative responsibility and controlling interpretive authority.
- Barnhart v. Walton: applied to permissible implementation within section 405(a) and its SSI incorporation; the same actual-delegation ground governs this different eligibility condition.
- Heimmermann v. First Union Mortgage Corp.: applied to an authorized interpretive mechanism consistent with statutory limits; no general power arises from ambiguity or expertise alone.
- Chevron U.S.A. Inc. v. Natural Resources Defense Council, Inc.: applied within the actual statutory assignment and permissible construction identified here.''',
'Law After Decision':'''The valid adult-disability sequence may stop at retained capacity for qualifying past relevant work. The later national-economy inquiry does not become an additional step-four condition. The decision applies existing delegated-choice doctrine to this statutory screen while preserving supported capacity findings, valid regulatory qualifications and ordinary review.''',
'Separate Writings':'',
'Procedure After Action':'''The case returns for implementation of the step-four rule and ordinary disposition of any remaining preserved capacity challenge. A labor-market survey is not required at that step; the denial stands unless a remaining challenge to the actual capacity determination succeeds.''',
'Source Notes':'''Barnhart v. Thomas, No. 02-763; 540 U.S. 20; Thomas v. Commissioner, 294 F.3d 568; 42 U.S.C. §§405(a), 423(d)(2)(A), 1382c(a)(3)(B) and 1383(d)(1); 20 C.F.R. §§404.1520 and 416.920. Thomas's individual job ended on August 25, 1995; nationwide extinction of the occupation was asserted rather than found.'''
}
